2026-10-04T02:27:18.001347Z TRACE tungstenite::protocol: Received message {"type": "response.output_item.done", "sequence_number": 410, "output_index": 2, "item": {"type": "message", "id": "msg_01aa896318fd1b12016ac1b98332e08199815f503a7e9c4c9f", "metadata": {"turn_id": "01a104bc-58e5-7e82-81c6-dbabcf618e4a"}, "internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough": {"create_time": 1791080835.2014046, "turn_id": "01a104bc-58e5-7e82-81c6-dbabcf618e4a"}, "role": "assistant", "status": "completed", "content": [{"type": "output_text", "text": "At dawn, the river gathers silver light beneath leaning willows. Its patient current carries leaves, reflections, and whispered birdsong downstream. Stones interrupt the flow, shaping ripples that brighten briefly everywhere. Along the bank, reeds bend together when cool winds arrive. A heron waits motionless, watching minnows flicker through green shadows.\n\nFarther upstream, rain enters through gullies cut across dark hills. Springs add colder water from hidden chambers beneath ancient limestone. Each tributary brings minerals, seeds, branches, and stories from elsewhere. The river accepts them all without surrendering its winding character. It widens near farms, where pumps hum beside flooded fields.\n\nChildren skip flat stones across calm pools after summer storms. Anglers study subtle circles, guessing where deep channels shelter trout. Bridges cross overhead, carrying bicycles, trucks, footsteps, and evening conversations. Beneath them, swallows stitch quick arcs above the moving surface. Their wings nearly touch water before rising toward open sky.\n\nIn autumn, maples release red leaves that drift like lanterns. Winter narrows the current beneath shelves of translucent blue ice. Spring breaks those edges, sending broken plates softly toward valleys. Summer warms shallow bends where deer drink cautiously at dusk. Through every season, the river changes yet remains recognizable somehow.\n\nTowns grew beside it, drawn by water, travel, and trade. Mills once turned its force into flour, cloth, and livelihoods. Later factories clouded stretches that had nourished generations of families. People eventually organized, cleaned banks, restored wetlands, and welcomed salmon. Recovery came slowly, measured in insects, otters, and clearer shallows.\n\nNear the sea, fresh water mingles with restless incoming tides. Mudflats spread widely, feeding crabs, shorebirds, grasses, and young fish. The river loosens into channels, then disappears within salt horizons. Still, clouds lift its water, returning rain to distant mountains. Thus the river completes no journey, only an endless exchange.\n\n2", "annotations": [], "logprobs": []}], "phase": "final_answer"}}
