2026-10-04T02:27:40.502643Z TRACE tungstenite::protocol: Received message {"type": "response.output_item.done", "item": {"id": "msg_021c72909a54ef2b016ac1b993584087d0bb213757d78e8ae2", "type": "message", "status": "completed", "content": [{"type": "output_text", "annotations": [], "logprobs": [], "text": "At dawn, the river slips out of the hills wearing a skin of silver. Alder roots grip its banks, and kingfishers wait on bent branches for the flash of minnows below. The current carries pine needles, fallen petals, and the cold memory of snowfields far upstream.\n\nBy noon, the river broadens beside farms and small towns. Children test the shallows with bare feet. Cattle stand knee deep near the reeds. On an old bridge, someone has carved two names into the rail, though rain and passing hands have softened every letter. Trucks cross above, briefly drowning the sound of water over stone.\n\nThe river keeps no single character. After storms, it turns brown and impatient, lifting branches and rolling rocks that seemed immovable. In summer, it shrinks into clear pools where trout hover beneath shadows. Winter seals the quiet coves with ice, while the central channel runs dark and steady.\n\nPeople call it by one name, but each bend feels like another place. One reach is quick and noisy. Another drifts through cottonwoods with barely a wrinkle. Near the coast, the water tastes the incoming tide and slows among mudflats bright with birds.\n\nAt evening, lamps appear along the levee. Their reflections tremble, break, and join again. The river passes abandoned mills, new apartments, boat ramps, orchards, and graveyards. It has carried timber, grain, ashes, sewage, wedding flowers, and generations of questions.\n\nPast midnight, under a moon blurred by cloud, the river continues toward the sea. It does not hurry, though everything within it moves. Stones become sand. Tributaries lose their names. The last freshwater reeds bow in the wind, and somewhere beyond them, waves wait to receive what the mountains began. Behind it, the valley sleeps, trusting the familiar sound along its banks through the long and dreaming darkness.\n\n1"}], "internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough": {"create_time": 1791080847.011651, "turn_id": "01a104bc-4851-7201-944b-e48a152ef67a"}, "phase": "final_answer", "role": "assistant", "metadata": {"turn_id": "01a104bc-4851-7201-944b-e48a152ef67a"}}, "output_index": 1, "sequence_number": 387}
